<title>SOUTH AFRICA: S.African July government financing 2.73 bln rand.</title>
<headline>S.African July government financing 2.73 bln rand.</headline>
<dateline>JOHANNESBURG 1997-08-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>South African government financing totalled 1.74 billion rand in July, bringing total financing to 20.24 billion rand so far this year, the Department of Finance said on Monday.</p>
<p>It said in a statement that financing of 2.73 billion rand was obtained through the issue of government bonds, while short-term loans decreased by 987.9 million rand.</p>
<p>				 Jul '97   Apr-July '97  Budget</p>
<p>								 1997/98</p>
<p>(Rand million)</p>
<p>Government bonds	   2,625.0   10,984.0	27,002.0</p>
<p>* Amortised interest</p>
<p>on zero coupon bonds	 102.0	548.4	   700.0</p>
<p>Short-term (net)</p>
<p>    Tender Bills	     594.2    2,332.1	 2,000.0</p>
<p>Corporation for</p>
<p>    Public Deposits     -1,582.1     -208.0	     0.0</p>
<p>Foreign loans		    0.0    3,823.8	 4,000.0</p>
<p>Transfer from IMF		0.0	345.3	 1,381.2</p>
<p>Proceeds from sale of</p>
<p>state assets		     0.0    1,165.4	   516.0</p>
<p>Transfer from Strategic</p>
<p>Fuel Fund			  0.0    1,250.0	 1,250.0</p>
<p>				--------   --------	--------</p>
<p>Total financing	    1,739.1   20,241.0	36,849.2</p>
<p>				--------   --------	--------</p>
<p>* The discount on zero coupon bonds is treated on an accrual basis. The discount is written off over the life of the loan and provided for annually as interest expenditure. At the same time a corresponding amount is added to new loans received by the Exchequer.</p>
<p>Government bonds to the amount of 387.2 million rand and foreign loans of 641.8 million rand were redeemed from April to July 1997, it added.</p>
<p>-- Johannesburg newsroom +27 11 482-1003</p>
